WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:04.140
JIM WALSH: The only
way the planet dies

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:09.690
in 30 minutes or less
today is if some man who

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:12.000
has control of thousands
of nuclear weapons

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.490
decides today is the day.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:17.580
SARAH HANSEN: Today on Chalk
Radio, the greatest threat

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.220
to human existence
and what people like

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.620
you and me can do about it.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.767
JIM WALSH: I often
hear people say

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:26.850
they can't imagine that
nuclear weapons would ever

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.820
be used because it's
a mutual suicide pact.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.090
Yeah, well, it's
important to remember

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:33.090
we used nuclear weapons.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.762
We've already used
them twice, right?

00:00:34.762 --> 00:00:36.720
So this is not something
that's never happened.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.480
It's something that's
happened, which should indicate

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.690
that it can happen again.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:44.040
SARAH HANSEN: I'm Sarah Hansen.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.530
And in this first
episode of season 4,

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:51.900
we're talking about course
8.S271 Nuclear Weapons: History

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:56.100
and Future Prospects, as well
as the MIT Nuclear Weapons

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.640
Education Project, which is a
collection of open materials

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.090
for lectures and discussions
about what nuclear weapons are

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.239
and their effects.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:08.390
So why is MIT teaching
about nuclear weapons?

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.580
And why is it important
that people like

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.890
you and me know about them?

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:15.350
To help explain all
this, I sat down

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.690
with two of the course
instructors-- one

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:21.523
a physicist and the
other a policy expert.

00:01:21.523 --> 00:01:23.690
BOB REDWINE: There are still
thousands and thousands

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:26.540
of nuclear weapons deployed
by the major powers.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:29.690
And the use of even a very
small fraction of them

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:33.230
would frankly end life
as we know it on Earth.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:34.940
It would end life.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:39.050
But the idea was to try to
bring this issue that we think

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.980
is existential to the
attention of young MIT

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:46.880
students, both undergraduates
and graduate students,

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.500
to get them interested in
it and working to continue

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.250
the efforts that had been going
on at MIT to try to get rid

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:56.530
of nuclear weapons.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:59.170
That was our motivation.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:01.960
SARAH HANSEN: That's Bob
Redwine, MIT emeritus professor

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.320
of physics and contributor
to the MIT Nuclear Weapons

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.480
Education Project.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:11.830
BOB REDWINE: In 1939, the
first controlled nuclear action

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:12.730
took place.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:16.300
And this was a real
revelation, in many ways,

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.540
to scientists in Europe
and the United States.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:22.900
There was a lot of
worry among scientists.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.860
One fear that became very
real to many scientists

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:31.000
was that Hitler and the
Nazis would get access

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:36.160
to this huge energy release that
we now know as nuclear weapons

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.610
before the Allies during
the second world war.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:44.110
So there was a big push
to have the Allies develop

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:46.840
nuclear weapons in
case the Nazis were

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.400
going to develop them first.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.770
And that was what
led to the Manhattan

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:55.563
Project that eventually led to
the two explosions in Japan.

00:02:55.563 --> 00:02:56.230
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.750
- Correspondents at air
headquarters on Guam

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:02.350
say there is reason to
believe that the Japanese city

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:05.440
of Hiroshima, approximately
the size of Memphis,

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.130
or Seattle, or Rochester,
New York, no longer exists.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:09.940
[END PLAYBACK]

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:13.930
SARAH HANSEN: On August
6, 1945, the United States

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:18.460
dropped a nuclear bomb on the
Japanese city of Hiroshima.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.790
Three days later, on
August 9, a second bomb

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:26.970
was dropped, this time
on the city of Nagasaki.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.030
Hundreds of thousands
of people were killed.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.670
It was total destruction.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:35.550
It's been nearly 80 years
since these attacks,

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:38.100
which remain the only
use of nuclear weapons

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.780
in armed conflict.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:43.020
Even so, following
the bombing of Japan,

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:45.540
many, many more
nuclear arms were

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:50.100
created by the United
States and the Soviet Union.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:52.380
JIM WALSH: The planet
fundamentally changed

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.900
in the summer of 1945.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.540
SARAH HANSEN: That's Jim Walsh,
senior research associate

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.480
at the MIT Security
Studies Program.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.940
JIM WALSH: We invented
this awful technology.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:06.007
And then our chief
adversary, the Soviet Union,

00:04:06.007 --> 00:04:07.590
who we thought was
trying to take over

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.780
the world, they got it, too.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.360
Our first impulse was to
build, and build, and build,

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.030
and build, both sides
approaching something

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:18.390
like 80,000 total nuclear
weapons between the two sides.

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:20.490
As Carl Sagan said,
it was like two people

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.680
sitting in a basement
filled with gasoline.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.540
One had 7,000 matches.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:25.560
One had 8,000.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.590
They argued over who
had more matches.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.950
What happened was, for the
first time in human history,

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.980
for the first time
in planetary history,

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.390
humans built a technology
that could destroy everything

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:40.140
on Earth.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:41.520
There are many
challenges that we

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.430
face that could pose
an extinction threat--

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:46.290
climate change
and other things--

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:48.210
that will play out over time.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:52.530
But the only way the planet
dies in 30 minutes or less

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:57.180
today is if some
man who has control

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:00.045
of thousands of nuclear weapons
decides today is the day.

00:05:05.937 --> 00:05:07.770
SARAH HANSEN: Jim
explained some of the ways

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.290
that the course goes beyond
the basic physical science

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:12.000
of nuclear weapons.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.010
He highlighted the
cultural, political,

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.650
and psychological implications
of their existence

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:18.810
and how, for a
long time, the fact

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:21.060
that countries like the
US and the Soviet Union

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:24.690
were stockpiling nuclear weapons
was of great concern to people

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.160
all over the globe.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.500
Both Jim and Bob
remember the days

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.380
when nuclear Armageddon was
one of the most common fears

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:33.120
of the general public.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.460
BOB REDWINE: I can
remember, in grade school,

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.490
we used to have regular
air raid drills,

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:40.320
where suddenly the
alarm would go off.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.600
And you would be
told to rush out

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.090
into the corridor
between classrooms

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.820
and put your head between
your knees up against the wall

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:50.160
until you were told to stop.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.550
And it's not obvious
how much good

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:55.710
that would do during
a nuclear explosion.

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:58.170
But that was what
the country was

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.723
accustomed to doing during
the Second World War.

00:06:01.723 --> 00:06:03.390
SARAH HANSEN: And
this tension of living

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:07.440
in fear of nuclear weapons
came to a peak in the 1960s

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.040
during the Cuban missile crisis,
when the US government warned

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:15.300
of a potential nuclear attack
by the Soviet Union from Cuba.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.000
BOB REDWINE: I happened
to be in high school

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.260
a few miles away from Washington
DC in northern Virginia.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:25.260
There was a time, like on
the order of a week or two,

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:28.860
when we weren't sure exactly
what was going to happen.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:31.770
Washington DC was
clearly a possible target

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:33.570
for such nuclear weapons.

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:35.910
It was frankly terrifying.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:39.420
We thought today might be
the last day of our lives.

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:44.440
SARAH HANSEN: Jim explained
that this fear, the thought

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:46.930
of nuclear attacks
and global extinction,

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:49.570
eventually led to action.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:51.910
JIM WALSH: At least two
periods in global history,

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:55.810
in the late 1950s and again
in the mid 1980s, citizens

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:58.990
and governments were
sufficiently scared

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:00.100
that they made changes.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:03.160
They actually destroyed the
nuclear weapons they built.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:06.310
They put themselves on a
path where things were safer

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:08.350
rather than more dangerous.

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:11.050
SARAH HANSEN: But this
progress, while meaningful,

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:12.515
was short-lived.

00:07:12.515 --> 00:07:14.140
JIM WALSH: Then the
end of the Cold War

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:16.840
came and we sort of
all forgot about it.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:18.730
SARAH HANSEN: But as
Bob went on to explain,

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:20.500
the threat hasn't disappeared.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:24.280
If anything, it's increased.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:26.290
BOB REDWINE: There are
currently many thousands

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:28.720
of nuclear weapons deployed.

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:31.780
As I think many of
you know, the power

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:35.230
of typical deployed nuclear
weapons now on average

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:39.190
is much greater than it was
in Hiroshima and Nagasaki.

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:42.520
If you had one of these
powerful nuclear weapons

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:45.370
exploding, let's
say, at a city, there

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:48.370
would be enormous
physical destruction.

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:50.515
It was enormous in
Hiroshima and Nagasaki.

00:07:50.515 --> 00:07:54.880
It would be even more
and many, many hundreds

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:56.590
of thousands of people killed.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:08:00.700
There would also be a
huge radiation problem

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:04.270
that would spread much beyond
the initial destruction

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:06.700
site that would
kill, in a reasonably

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:11.290
short time, many more
people from the radiation.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:16.330
And in addition, there would be
a serious long-term radiation

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:19.870
and climate change issue
spreading around the Earth

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:23.650
from this explosion over the
months and years following

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:24.730
that explosion.

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:27.520
That's if there were
just one explosion.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:34.299
If we had 20 to 50 explosions
of this sort anywhere on Earth,

00:08:34.299 --> 00:08:38.230
it would basically end life
as we know it on Earth.

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:40.240
And we've got
thousands of weapons

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:41.890
deployed and ready to go.

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:47.782
JIM WALSH: I often
hear people say,

00:08:47.782 --> 00:08:49.740
I can't imagine that
nuclear weapons would ever

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:52.650
be used because it's
a mutual suicide pact.

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:54.990
Yeah, well, it's
important to remember

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:55.980
we use nuclear weapons.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:58.260
We've already used them twice.

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:00.420
As a political scientist
who studies deterrence

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:03.120
and the possible deployment
of nuclear weapons,

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:06.270
let me remind listeners
it's been US policy

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:10.560
to use nuclear weapons first in
a normal, conventional conflict

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.900
throughout the decades
of the Cold War.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:16.410
Just as we fret today that
Russia might introduce nuclear

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:19.590
weapons because they're
losing a conventional war--

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:23.010
a war of tanks, and artillery,
and that sort of thing--

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:24.480
that was the US doctrine.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.790
So all during the Cold
War, the US doctrine

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:31.290
was the Soviets had more
tanks and stuff than we

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.000
did along the European front.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.520
We didn't have
enough men and arms

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:37.500
to repel a massive invasion.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.830
So if they invaded us
with their tanks and guns

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:43.740
and we started to lose,
we'd use nuclear weapons.

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.313
That is our policy.

00:09:45.313 --> 00:09:46.980
The current policy
of the United States,

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:49.560
its current nuclear
doctrine is counterforce.

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:51.540
It's not mutually
assured destruction.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:54.000
It's that we're going
to hit you first and try

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.100
to take out all
your nuclear weapons

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:58.230
so that you don't hit us--

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:03.450
an incredibly dangerous
and destabilizing policy.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:06.210
It's not I'll hit you
back if you hit me.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.550
It's I'm coming
after you because I

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:09.960
think I can get you first.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.930
So imagine how the
adversary feels in that.

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:15.270
These are just simple human
beings, like you and me.

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:18.600
It's not like the human race has
matured and is suddenly wiser

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:20.460
just because we happened
to invent something

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:21.840
that could kill everything.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:25.260
No, we basically
shoved that technology

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:27.750
into our normal way of
thinking about things.

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:30.150
We built tons and tons
of nuclear weapons.

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:31.200
They did the same.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:32.670
We had lots of tests.

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:36.810
They did the same because it was
considered a normal instrument

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:39.210
in a fight between
two adversaries

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:42.330
with the occasional second
thought-- oh my god,

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:47.190
are we on the verge
of killing everything?

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:49.290
SARAH HANSEN: While all
of this is terrifying,

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:52.440
it's also why the MIT
Nuclear Weapons Education

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:56.550
Project and the course
8.S271 were created

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:59.340
and why they're shared
on MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:02.520
The more we understand nuclear
weapons and their effects,

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:04.440
the better equipped
we'll be to advocate

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:06.550
for their dismantlement.

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:09.400
Much of the course is built
around class discussion.

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:11.260
The instructors
focus on developing

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:13.330
students' understanding
of the technology

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:15.640
and the political context
in which decisions

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:18.297
about the technology
are being made.

00:11:18.297 --> 00:11:20.380
JIM WALSH: Nuclear weapons
is a topic that invites

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:22.600
an interdisciplinary approach.

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:25.840
When we try to understand the
choices that humans have made

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:28.030
about nuclear weapons,
we need to understand

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:29.960
what are their political
interests, what's

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:31.960
the nature of the
international competition they

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:33.370
find themselves in.

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:35.410
What about bureaucratic battles?

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:37.480
I think morally it's a
pretty easy decision.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:41.170
I don't think anyone ever
has or will have the right

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:42.490
to extinguish life on Earth.

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:44.110
I don't think any human has it.

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:47.470
But as a policy matter, I can
imagine there are debates.

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:50.500
And so for example,
if Russia uses

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:53.860
nuclear weapons in
Ukraine, should the US

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:55.482
respond with nuclear weapons?

00:11:55.482 --> 00:11:57.190
And I would say the
majority of the class

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:59.565
said, no, we shouldn't use
nuclear weapons because Russia

00:11:59.565 --> 00:12:00.065
does.

00:12:00.065 --> 00:12:01.720
And I'd be inclined
to agree with that.

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:03.928
But let's be honest.

00:12:03.928 --> 00:12:04.720
Let's say we don't.

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:07.750
Does that encourage Russia
to use them a second time?

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:09.820
Does it send a message
to other countries

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.460
that they can use nuclear
weapons with impunity?

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:16.480
Very few of these choices
in the nuclear domain

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:18.490
are without tradeoffs
and challenges.

00:12:18.490 --> 00:12:20.080
And so we have to
be honest about that

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:22.180
as we teach the course
and let students

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:26.260
put their chips on what they
think is the best path forward.

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:29.020
SARAH HANSEN: And herein lies
maybe the most important aspect

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:32.860
of the course, showing
students and really all of us

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:36.850
that we do have a say in our
future with nuclear weapons.

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:39.160
BOB REDWINE: I think
we collectively

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:42.010
as humans have no
choice but to try

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:43.660
to get rid of all
nuclear weapons

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:46.540
because as we
discussed, they do pose

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:48.370
such an existential threat.

00:12:48.370 --> 00:12:51.850
It's not easy, given
the current situation.

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:53.800
I think we have
to do the best we

00:12:53.800 --> 00:12:59.290
can to educate people who are
not experts about the dangers

00:12:59.290 --> 00:13:04.300
and to encourage them to vote
and to influence their leaders

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:06.600
in the right direction.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.220
JIM WALSH: Anyone
under the age of 50

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:13.350
has not lived a minute,
not 60 seconds in a world

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:16.920
where all around them the most
important issue of the day

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:17.850
was nuclear weapons.

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:19.200
You've never experienced that.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:21.968
For decades, we've been told
it's the government's job, as

00:13:21.968 --> 00:13:23.760
if the governments that
own nuclear weapons

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:26.820
are going to get rid of their
own unless they're pushed.

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:29.010
Not likely!

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:31.830
But when you say that
for decade after decade

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:34.590
and the memory fades, then you
find yourself in the position

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:35.370
we are today.

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:37.170
Everyone thinks it's
a terrible problem.

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:39.003
No one thinks they can
do anything about it.

00:13:39.003 --> 00:13:42.000
And that is a problem
in their heads.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:44.130
The only thing that
prevents us from doing it

00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:46.620
is the decision we make.

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:49.302
And so if we make a
different decision,

00:13:49.302 --> 00:13:50.760
then we'll get a
different outcome.

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:51.802
But that's the challenge.

00:13:51.802 --> 00:13:54.600
It's the-- it's the
psychological challenge that's

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:56.523
the toughest when it
comes to nuclear weapons.

00:13:56.523 --> 00:13:59.190
And that's why it's important to
remember that we've had success

00:13:59.190 --> 00:14:00.482
and we can be successful again.

